     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. 20 mg is twice the FDA-labeled maximum for amlodipine, which is 10 mg once daily. Higher doses increase the risk of significant hypotension, reflex tachycardia, and peripheral edema, and they usually add little extra blood pressure control.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **Start low:** 5 mg PO daily is the usual starting dose. Some patients, such as frail or elderly patients or those with hepatic impairment, start at 2.5 mg.\n- **Titrate up:** If the patient needs more, increase to the 10 mg maximum after 1\u20132 weeks, depending on response.\n- **Add a second agent:** If 10 mg isn't enough, an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or thiazide-type diuretic is generally preferred over going above the maximum.\n\nIf 20 mg was a typo, tell me the dose you want. For example, I can enter amlodipine 5 mg or 10 mg PO once daily. If you really do intend 20 mg, please confirm that with your supervising resident or attending and the pharmacist. I can then enter it with a note documenting that approval."
      }
     ],
